Until recently, gorillas were considered to be a single species, with three subspecies: the western lowland gorilla, the eastern lowland gorilla and the mountain gorilla.<ref name="Groves2002">{{Cite book |first=C. |last=Groves |author-link=Colin Groves |year=2002 |chapter-url=http://arts.anu.edu.au/grovco/Gorilla%20Biology.pdf |chapter=A history of gorilla taxonomy |title=Gorilla biology: A multidisciplinary perspective |editor1-first=A. B. |editor1-last=Taylor |editor2-first=M. L. |editor2-last=Goldsmith |publisher=Cambridge University Press |pages=15–34 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090326001207/http://arts.anu.edu.au/grovco/Gorilla%20Biology.pdf |archive-date=26 March 2009}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.fathom.com/course/21701783/ |title=Mountain gorillas of the Virungas |access-date=11 September 2008 |last1=Stewart |first1=K. J. |first2=P. |last2=Sicotte |first3=M. M. |last3=Robbins |year=2001 |publisher=Fathom / Cambridge University Press |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080921145524/http://www.fathom.com/course/21701783/ |archive-date=21 September 2008}}</ref> There is now agreement that there are two species, each with two subspecies.<ref name="Mittermeier_etal2013">{{cite book |editor1-last=Mittermeier |editor1-first=R. A. |editor1-link=Russell A. Mittermeier |editor2-first=A. B. |editor2-last=Rylands |editor3-first=D. E. |editor3-last=Wilson |editor3-link=Don E. Wilson |title=Handbook of the Mammals of the World. Volume 3. Primates |publisher=Lynx Edicions |location=Barcelona |year=2013 |isbn=978-84-96553-89-7}}</ref> More recently, a third subspecies has been claimed to exist in one of the species. The separate species and subspecies developed from a single type of gorilla during the [[Last Glacial Period|Ice Age]], when their forest habitats shrank and became isolated from each other.<ref name="Prince-Hughes66">{{Cite book |title=Songs of the gorilla nation |last=Prince-Hughes |first=D. |author-link=Dawn Prince-Hughes |year=1987 |publisher=Harmony |isbn= 978-1-4000-5058-1 |page= [https://archive.org/details/songsofgorillana00prinrich/page/66 66] |url= https://archive.org/details/songsofgorillana00prinrich/page/66}} {{bsn|date=December 2024|reason=even if the thought-provoking autobiographical memoir "Songs of the Gorilla Nation: My journey through autism" happens to support somewhere all the claims cited to it (added by [[User:Gloriamarie]] in unbroken sequence beginning [[Special:Diff/373104341]] in July 2010), we still probably need a real source for them. Also page 66 is a poem not even self-evidently about gorillas.}}</ref>{{Failed verification|date=September 2024}} [[Primatologists]] continue to explore the relationships between various gorilla populations.<ref name="Groves2002"/> The species and subspecies listed here are the ones upon which most scientists agree.<ref name=MSW3 /><ref name="Mittermeier_etal2013" />
