The proposed third subspecies of ''Gorilla beringei'', which has not yet received a [[trinomen]], is the Bwindi population of the mountain gorilla, sometimes called the [[Bwindi Impenetrable National Park#Mountain gorillas|Bwindi gorilla]].

Some variations that distinguish the classifications of gorilla include varying density, size, hair colour, length, culture, and facial widths.<ref name="Prince-Hughes66"/> Population genetics of the lowland gorillas suggest that the western and eastern lowland populations diverged around 261 thousand years ago.<ref name="McManus et al 2015">{{cite journal |doi=10.1093/molbev/msu394 |pmid=25534031 |pmc=4327160 |title=Inference of gorilla demographic and selective history from whole-genome sequence data |journal=Molecular Biology and Evolution |volume=32 |issue=3 |pages=600–612 |year=2015 |last1=McManus |first1=K. F. |last2=Kelley |first2=J. L. |last3=Song |first3=S.
